            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Let's Write Some Code!</h2>
                    <p>In this lesson, you'll create your first C# program from scratch. We'll explore the traditional "Hello, World!" program, understand the structure of C# code, and learn how to build and run your applications.</p>
                    
                    <p>By the end of this lesson, you'll be comfortable creating new projects and understanding what each part of a C# program does.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Creating Your First Project</h2>
                    <p>Let's create a new C# console application using the .NET CLI. Open your terminal or command prompt and follow these steps:</p>

                    <h3>Step 1: Create a New Project</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Create a new console application
dotnet new console -n HelloWorld

# Navigate into the project folder
cd HelloWorld</code></pre>

                    <p>This command creates a new folder called <code>HelloWorld</code> with the following structure:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash">HelloWorld/
├── HelloWorld.csproj    # Project configuration file
├── Program.cs           # Your main code file
└── obj/                 # Build artifacts (auto-generated)</code></pre>

                    <h3>Step 2: Open the Project</h3>
                    <p>Open the <code>HelloWorld</code> folder in Visual Studio Code:</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Open in VS Code
code .</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Tip</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>If VS Code asks you to add required assets for build and debug, click "Yes" to enable debugging support.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Understanding the Generated Code</h2>
                    <p>Open <code>Program.cs</code> and you'll see this simple code:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Program.cs (Modern .NET 6+ style)
Console.WriteLine("Hello, World!");</code></pre>

                    <p>Wait, that's it? Just one line? Yes! Modern .NET uses <strong>top-level statements</strong> which remove all the boilerplate code. But let's understand what's happening behind the scenes.</p>

                    <h3>The Traditional Structure</h3>
                    <p>Before .NET 6, the same program looked like this:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">using System;

namespace HelloWorld
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Hello, World!");
        }
    }
}</code></pre>

                    <p>Let's break down each part:</p>

                    <div class="code-explanation">
                        <div class="explanation-item">
                            <code>using System;</code>
                            <p>Imports the <code>System</code> namespace, giving us access to <code>Console</code> and other fundamental types. In modern .NET, this is automatically included via "implicit usings."</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="explanation-item">
                            <code>namespace HelloWorld</code>
                            <p>A namespace is a container that organizes your code and prevents naming conflicts. Think of it like a folder for your classes.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="explanation-item">
                            <code>class Program</code>
                            <p>A class is a blueprint for creating objects. Every C# program needs at least one class. <code>Program</code> is the conventional name for the main class.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="explanation-item">
                            <code>static void Main(string[] args)</code>
                            <p>The <strong>Main</strong> method is the entry point of your application – where execution begins. <code>static</code> means it belongs to the class itself, not an instance. <code>void</code> means it doesn't return a value. <code>string[] args</code> receives command-line arguments.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="explanation-item">
                            <code>Console.WriteLine("Hello, World!");</code>
                            <p>This line prints text to the console. <code>Console</code> is a class, <code>WriteLine</code> is a method that outputs text followed by a new line.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Running Your Program</h2>
                    <p>Now let's run the program! In your terminal, make sure you're in the project folder and run:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash">dotnet run</code></pre>

                    <p>You should see:</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">Hello, World!</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box success">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Congratulations!</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>You've just written and executed your first C# program! 🎉</p>
                    </div>

                    <h3>What Happened?</h3>
                    <p>When you run <code>dotnet run</code>, several things happen:</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li><strong>Compilation:</strong> The C# compiler converts your code into Intermediate Language (IL)</li>
                        <li><strong>Build:</strong> The IL is packaged into a DLL file</li>
                        <li><strong>Execution:</strong> The .NET runtime loads and executes your program</li>
                    </ol>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Making It Interactive</h2>
                    <p>Let's modify our program to be more interactive. Update <code>Program.cs</code>:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// A more interactive Hello World
Console.WriteLine("Welcome to C#!");
Console.WriteLine("==================");

// Ask for the user's name
Console.Write("What is your name? ");
string name = Console.ReadLine();

// Greet the user
Console.WriteLine($"Hello, {name}! Nice to meet you.");
Console.WriteLine("You've just written your first interactive C# program!");</code></pre>

                    <p>Run it again with <code>dotnet run</code>:</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">Welcome to C#!
==================
What is your name? Alice
Hello, Alice! Nice to meet you.
You've just written your first interactive C# program!</code></pre>

                    <h3>New Concepts Introduced</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>Console.Write()</code> – Prints text without adding a new line (cursor stays on the same line)</li>
                        <li><code>Console.ReadLine()</code> – Waits for the user to type something and press Enter, then returns the text</li>
                        <li><code>string name</code> – Declares a variable called <code>name</code> that stores text</li>
                        <li><code>$"Hello, {name}!"</code> – String interpolation: inserts the value of <code>name</code> into the string</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Comments in C#</h2>
                    <p>Comments are notes in your code that the compiler ignores. They help explain what your code does.</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// This is a single-line comment

/* This is a 
   multi-line comment
   that spans several lines */

/// &lt;summary&gt;
/// This is an XML documentation comment.
/// Used to document classes, methods, etc.
/// &lt;/summary&gt;

Console.WriteLine("Comments are ignored by the compiler");</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Best Practice</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>Write comments to explain <em>why</em> you're doing something, not <em>what</em> you're doing. The code itself should be clear enough to show what it does.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>The Project File (.csproj)</h2>
                    <p>Let's take a quick look at the project file. Open <code>HelloWorld.csproj</code>:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk"&gt;

  &lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
    &lt;OutputType&gt;Exe&lt;/OutputType&gt;
    &lt;TargetFramework&gt;net10.0&lt;/TargetFramework&gt;
    &lt;ImplicitUsings&gt;enable&lt;/ImplicitUsings&gt;
    &lt;Nullable&gt;enable&lt;/Nullable&gt;
  &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;

&lt;/Project&gt;</code></pre>

                    <p>Key settings explained:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>OutputType</code> – <code>Exe</code> means this is an executable program (not a library)</li>
                        <li><code>TargetFramework</code> – The .NET version this project uses</li>
                        <li><code>ImplicitUsings</code> – Automatically includes common namespaces like <code>System</code></li>
                        <li><code>Nullable</code> – Enables nullable reference types (helps prevent null errors)</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Common .NET CLI Commands</h2>
                    <p>Here are the essential commands you'll use frequently:</p>

                    <div class="command-table">
                        <div class="command-row">
                            <code>dotnet new console -n MyApp</code>
                            <span>Create a new console application</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="command-row">
                            <code>dotnet build</code>
                            <span>Compile the project</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="command-row">
                            <code>dotnet run</code>
                            <span>Build and run the project</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="command-row">
                            <code>dotnet clean</code>
                            <span>Remove build outputs</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="command-row">
                            <code>dotnet --info</code>
                            <span>Show .NET installation details</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="command-row">
                            <code>dotnet new list</code>
                            <span>List available project templates</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use <code>dotnet new console</code> to create a new console application.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Modern C# uses top-level statements to reduce boilerplate code.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway><code>Console.WriteLine()</code> prints output; <code>Console.ReadLine()</code> reads input.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>String interpolation (<code>$"..."</code>) lets you embed variables in strings.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use <code>dotnet run</code> to compile and execute your program.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>

